  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1126 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nSaturday, the upper ridge passes east of the area with increasing \nupper level southwesterly flow ahead of a deepening wave over the \nIntermountain West. This will continue to advect in milder \ntemperatures and we will also see increasing moisture. This moisture \nadvection will be well above normal for this time of year as a plume \nof moisture from the Gulf brings in PWATs in excess of 1.00 inch, \nwith the deterministic ECMWF having values above the climatological \nmax at 1.20 inches! While it is possible that this value will not be \nachieved, the signal suggests that a bountiful influx of moisture \nwill help saturate the column and support rainfall potential with \nthis system late Saturday into Sunday AM.\n\nLighter rainfall accumulations and an isolated rumble or two of\nthunder are expected with this system Saturday evening through \nSunday. Both PoPs and QPF remain similar to the last forecast \npackage, with increasing confidence in a quick-hitting but \nwidespread light-moderate rainfall and PoPs of 70-100% as the \nfront passes through Saturday night. Chances for rainfall \ngreater than 0.25\" remain around 50-90%, with the greatest \nchances in central and eastern Iowa. Will continue to monitor \ntrends in rainfall amounts and coverage, as some guidance \ncontinues to show the potential for upwards to a 0.50\" or higher\nfor some. Best chances (30-50%) for these higher accumulation \ntotals are currently favored along and north of a line from \nFairfield to Dubuque IA. \n\nAs the aforementioned system clears the area Sunday, cooler \ntemperatures are expected. This will bring us down to near-normal or \neven below normal temps for the coming week. In addition, as flow \naloft becomes more zonal, a progressive pattern with multiple waves \ncould set the stage for an active stretch of weather.",
